;;; std.core — a state that holds `(false)` is dead, by a rule.
;;;
;;; ── What the rule says ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   (false)  ⟹  the state is inconsistent
;;;
;;; `std.core.inconsistent` is in every program, with no import line
;;; (Q-M1f.551). It matches a held `(false)` and states `:ein inconsistent`, a
;;; word to the engine and no fact (Q-M1f.399): where it fires, the saturation
;;; of the state ends and the state is dead. `(false)` itself is a fact like
;;; any other, which the rule below concludes.
;;;
;;; ── The program ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A switch is on and off at once, which `on-and-off` calls `(false)`. Nothing
;;; here is supposed: root derives the `(false)`, the module's rule fires on
;;; it, and root is dead.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation means ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; R is empty: `(false)`, the claim's word for it. Take the module's rule
;;; away and root would hold `(false)` as a fact and be alive, R root's one
;;; state, and the claim would fail. The dead root's core is the two facts
;;; `on-and-off` matched, and it is refuted by `on-and-off`: an explanation
;;; reads through the module's rule to the rule that concluded the `(false)`.

(relation on  Switch :why "{?3} is on")
(relation off Switch :why "{?3} is off")

(on  s1)
(off s1)

(rule on-and-off ()
  :match  (and (on ?s) (off ?s))
  :assert (false)
  :why    "{?s} is on and off at once")

(solve :expect (false))
